<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles III of Navarre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1361">1361</a>–1425</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Charles III the Noble</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles III the Noble was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> from 1387, of the French house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a>. Unlike his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-navarre">Charles the Bad</a>, he kept peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, exchanging his Norman claims for the duchy of Nemours. He reorganised the kingdom's administration, united the boroughs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> in 1423, and built the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olite">Olite</a> and the cathedral cloister of Pamplona.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-navarre.jpg" alt="Charles III of Navarre" width="320" height="508" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karel3_Evreux_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was born on 22 July 1361 at Mantes, the heart of his father's lands in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the firstborn son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-navarre">Charles II of Navarre</a> and the French princess Joan of Valois. The House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evreux">Évreux</a> was a cadet branch of the French royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty">House of Capet</a>. As a young man he was often sent by his father as envoy to France. On 27 May 1375, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, he married Eleanor, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-castile">Henry II of Castile</a>, which sealed the peace between their fathers.</p><p>In 1378 an embassy to King Charles V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ended in his arrest. During interrogation he revealed his father's plan to seize the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logrono">Logroño</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and forced the Treaty of Briones. Eleanor helped secure his release in 1381.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Charles became king on 1 January 1387 and the same year recovered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> the last occupied parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. His early reign was marked by a breach with Eleanor, who returned to Castile with their daughters and stayed away for seven years. The historian Elena Woodacre suggests the cause was his illegitimate children, since Eleanor found four mistresses and six such children at court. The couple were reunited in November 1394 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>.</p><p>Charles was an effective diplomat. In 1393 he secured the return of Cherbourg from the English. Through the marriages of his sisters and legitimate daughters to Iberian and southern French rulers and nobles, and of his illegitimate children into the Navarrese nobility, he built alliances that kept the peace. Until 1406 he took an active part in French affairs, after which he turned towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. In 1416 he stopped recognising Benedict XIII at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> and accepted Gregory XII at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Both of Charles's sons died in 1402, and his eldest daughter Joan died in 1413, so his third daughter Blanche became heir. In October 1419 she married John, son of the king of Aragon, after Charles promised not to remarry and father sons who might displace her. On 8 September 1425 Charles collapsed suddenly, died the same day in Blanche's arms and was buried in the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> beside Eleanor, who had died in 1416. Historians debate whether he saw himself chiefly as a French prince or an Iberian king, but agree that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> became steadily more Iberian under him. He earned the name the Noble in contrast to his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-navarre">Charles the Bad</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles III of Navarre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Charles III the Noble</b> (1387–1425) built the palace of Olite and in 1423 united the three rival boroughs of Pamplona by the Privilege of the Union.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1404 his son <b>Charles III of Navarre</b> exchanged Évreux with Charles VI for the duchy of Nemours, and it returned to the Kingdom of France.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-evreux">County of Évreux</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olite">Olite/Erriberri</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.22,-1.55&amp;year=1402" aria-label="Nantes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4514 507 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles III of Navarre"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4964 777h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4964 777h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.22,-1.55&amp;year=1402">What was here in 1402? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
